    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start for an intro, but the ranking tool just flagged it as a \"low engagement\" draft because it didn't hit enough of the instructional markers. It's looking for more of those \"helpful/learn/understand\" cues to make sure the tone is right for the students. \n\nCan you expand on this? Maybe break down the actual stages (Prophase, Metaphase, etc.) but try to make the explanation more \"instructional.\" Just don't make it weird or repetitive—I really don't want the students to think it's written by a robot.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To really understand mitosis, think of it in four key stages. First, in Prophase, the messy DNA coils up into visible chromosomes. Next, Metaphase lines up these chromosomes neatly along the cell's center. Anaphase then pulls the copied chromosomes apart to opposite sides. Finally, in Telophase, the cell splits completely, allowing two new cells to form, which helps students learn how identical copies are made.",
      "source": "target",
